 "drivingQuestion": "From where is it derived that appointing lesser courts requires 71?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "derived from Moses' practice",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "sanhedrin.16b.9",
   "daf": "16b",
   "sentenceId": 8,
   "he": "וְאֵין עוֹשִׂין סַנְהֶדְרָאוֹת כּוּ׳. מְנָא לַן? כִּדְאַשְׁכְּחַן בְּמֹשֶׁה, דְּאוֹקִי סַנְהֶדְרָאוֹת, וּמֹשֶׁה בִּמְקוֹם שִׁבְעִים וְחַד קָאֵי.",
   "en": "§ The mishna teaches that they may «appoint» a lesser «Sanhedrin» for the tribes only on the basis of a court of seventy-one judges. The Gemara asks: «From where do we» derive this matter? The Gemara answers: It is «as we find with regard to Moses, who established» lesser «courts» for all of the people (see Exodus 18:25–26), «and Moses stands in place of» the «seventy-one» judges on the Great Sanhedrin. Consequently, a lesser Sanhedrin that stands at the head of a tribe is appointed by the Great Sanhedrin."
  }
 ],
